You are given an array of integers stones where stones[i] is the weight of the ith stone.
We are playing a game with the stones. On each turn, we choose any two stones and smash them together.
Suppose the stones have weights x and y with x <= y. The result of this smash is:
If x == y, both stones are destroyed, and
If x != y, the stone of weight x is destroyed, and the stone of weight y has new weight y - x.
At the end of the game, there is at most one stone left.
Return the smallest possible weight of the left stone. If there are no stones left, return 0.

Example 1:
Input: stones = [2,7,4,1,8,1]
Output: 1
Explanation:
We can combine 2 and 4 to get 2, so the array converts to [2,7,1,8,1] then,
we can combine 7 and 8 to get 1, so the array converts to [2,1,1,1] then,
we can combine 2 and 1 to get 1, so the array converts to [1,1,1] then,
we can combine 1 and 1 to get 0, so the array converts to [1], then that's the optimal value.

Example 2:
Input: stones = [31,26,33,21,40]
Output: 5

Constraints:
1 <= stones.length <= 30
1 <= stones[i] <= 100

Approach:
This problem is nothing but Partition a Set Into Two Subsets with Min Difference in disguise. To learn an another
approach in solving such type of problems the other approach is used here. Instead of finding the closest subset
to totalSum/2 here we find whether for every sum from 0 to totalSum/2 if a subset exists, if so we mark it
true otherwise as false. Later a scan is carried from totalSum/2 to 0 for the nth row to find the max subset which
when caught the scan ends. There are two base cases here one when the capacity or the sum is 0 then we return true as
we come to a conclusion that a subset is present for this sum and from here we start building the answer even though
there never comes a sum = 0 according to the constraints. The second base is when the number of elements go to 0 then
we return false as the capacity still exists.

Naive approach -> TC = O(sum/2 * 2^n) = O(sum * 2^n), SC = O(n) (recursion stack space)
Each call makes up to 2 recursive calls, and every recursive call can goes n levels deep. Also, an extra loop is required
to check all the sums(i.e sum/2)
Memoization -> TC = O(n*sum), SC = O(n*sum) + O(n) (recursion stack space) = O(n*sum)
Even though there is an extra loop for the sum. Every sum uses the same dp and does not visit the already visited cells.
Tabulation -> TC = O(n*sum), SC = O(n*sum)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation_1 -> TC = O(n*sum), SC = O(2*sum) = O(sum)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation_2 -> TC = O(n*sum), SC = O(sum)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation_3 -> TC = O(n*sum), SC = O(sum)